--TEST--
`Core\Cache\Store::forget` takes the entry under a name out of the store it was named on, on every tier, and leaves the name ordinary rather than spent — `rule:concurrency/cross-request-state-is-explicit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
var $cache = Core\Cache::memory();

$cache->put("receipt", "written");
echo "written: ", $cache->get("receipt") != null ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The second reason an entry goes, after the cap and the lifetime that are the
// tier's own: this one is the program saying which entry, by name.
$cache->forget("receipt");
echo "forgotten: ", $cache->get("receipt") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// The name is not spent — forgetting takes the entry out, not the key — so a
// later `put` under it reads back like any other.
$cache->put("receipt", "written again");
var $again = ($cache->get("receipt") ?? "") as string;
echo "and the name is reusable: ", $again, "\n";

// The shared tier forgets over the wire, with the same answer — none — and a
// deployment that configured no store is told so by name.
try {
    Core\Cache::shared();
    echo "shared handed back a store\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo "the shared tier forgets over the wire, and this deployment has no store\n";
}
--EXPECT--
written: yes
forgotten: null
and the name is reusable: written again
the shared tier forgets over the wire, and this deployment has no store
